  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nA weak frontal boundary has pushed further south and west into \nthe forecast area as of early this morning, and this will bring a\ncorresponding shift in the dangerous heat threat alignment for \ntoday. The greater heat danger threat will be generally along/west\nof the I-55 corridor, and we have maintained a heat advisory with\njust some minor adjustments to expand it slightly eastward. In\nterms of convective rainfall for today, a slight mid level warming\nand reduced lapse rates makes today's airmass not quite as\nfavorable for convective development and microburst potential.\n \nGoing through the remainder of the week through the weekend, the\nweather pattern will remain mostly unchanged, and this means that\nthe semi-permanent subtropical ridge will keep temperatures above\nnormal for the foreseeable future. It is worth noting however \nthat a subtle weakness in the ridge is forecast to develop over \nthe lower Mississippi Valley region during the Thursday through \nSaturday time frame, and while this will not do much to alleviate \nthe heat, it should allow for greater coverage of diurnal \nconvection. Looking ahead to next week, the bulk of global model \nguidance indicate the ridge will have no trouble re- intensifying \nto prior levels, and then some, which means we will once again be \nfacing additional heat stress concerns. /EC/",
